		<apod:explanation><![CDATA[<strong>Explanation: </strong>After this galaxy battle, only one galaxy will remain. Already visible in the <a href="https://app.astrobin.com/u/Bluesky71?i=vn4g73">featured picture</a> are <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-june-1-ugc-1810-wildly-interacting-galaxy-from-hubble/">rings</a> and faint star <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-may-17-ngc-1316-after-galaxies-collide/">swirls</a> from previous <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-may-14-galaxy-collisions-simulation-vs-observations/">gravitational encounters</a>. Known collectively as the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-august-31-ifn-and-the-ngc-7771-group/">NGC 7771 Group</a>, these galaxies lie almost 200 million <a href="https://chandra.harvard.edu/resources/faq/astrophysics/astrophysics-18.html">light-years</a> away toward the high-flying <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pegasus_(constellation)">constellation Pegasus</a>. The largest galaxy, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barred_spiral_galaxy">barred spiral</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7771">NGC 7771</a> on the lower right, is itself about 75,000 light-years across, and the galaxy on the upper left is spiral <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7769">NGC 7769</a>, which is almost as large. As these and other <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_galaxy">smaller galaxies</a> make repeated close passages, they will <a href="https://i.pinimg.com/736x/3b/fd/fe/3bfdfefb4f45fc0d90af3a111e2704b5.jpg">all likely merge</a> into one very large galaxy. Playing out over billions of years, the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-highlights/galaxy-details-and-mergers/">merger process</a> is understood to be a normal part of the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/evolution/">evolution of galaxies</a>. Studying <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-january-6-colliding-spiral-galaxies-from-webb-and-hubble/">galaxy clashes</a> like this helps <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-august-18-human-as-spaceship/">humanity</a> to better understand the <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-025-02563-1">possible</a> merger <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-june-6-milky-way-galaxy-doomed-collision-with-andromeda-pending/">between</a> our own <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/%22">Milky Way</a> and <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-15-andromeda-and-friends/">Andromeda</a> galaxies.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>open space]]></apod:explanation>
		<description><![CDATA[<strong>Explanation: </strong>After this galaxy battle, only one galaxy will remain. Already visible in the <a href="https://app.astrobin.com/u/Bluesky71?i=vn4g73">featured picture</a> are <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-june-1-ugc-1810-wildly-interacting-galaxy-from-hubble/">rings</a> and faint star <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-may-17-ngc-1316-after-galaxies-collide/">swirls</a> from previous <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-may-14-galaxy-collisions-simulation-vs-observations/">gravitational encounters</a>. Known collectively as the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-august-31-ifn-and-the-ngc-7771-group/">NGC 7771 Group</a>, these galaxies lie almost 200 million <a href="https://chandra.harvard.edu/resources/faq/astrophysics/astrophysics-18.html">light-years</a> away toward the high-flying <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pegasus_(constellation)">constellation Pegasus</a>. The largest galaxy, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barred_spiral_galaxy">barred spiral</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7771">NGC 7771</a> on the lower right, is itself about 75,000 light-years across, and the galaxy on the upper left is spiral <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7769">NGC 7769</a>, which is almost as large. As these and other <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_galaxy">smaller galaxies</a> make repeated close passages, they will <a href="https://i.pinimg.com/736x/3b/fd/fe/3bfdfefb4f45fc0d90af3a111e2704b5.jpg">all likely merge</a> into one very large galaxy. Playing out over billions of years, the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-highlights/galaxy-details-and-mergers/">merger process</a> is understood to be a normal part of the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/evolution/">evolution of galaxies</a>. Studying <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-january-6-colliding-spiral-galaxies-from-webb-and-hubble/">galaxy clashes</a> like this helps <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-august-18-human-as-spaceship/">humanity</a> to better understand the <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-025-02563-1">possible</a> merger <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-june-6-milky-way-galaxy-doomed-collision-with-andromeda-pending/">between</a> our own <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/%22">Milky Way</a> and <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-15-andromeda-and-friends/">Andromeda</a> galaxies.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>open space]]></description>
